{
  "image_name": "42254_2025_914_Fig2_HTML.webp",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how fermionic dynamics are turned into a quantum-computable simulation. It starts with a fermionic Hamiltonian \\(H\\), which is mapped by a chosen representation into a qubit Hamiltonian \\(\\tilde H\\). The top of the figure shows the target task: evolving an input state \\(|\\psi\\rangle\\) under fermionic time evolution to produce \\(e^{-itH}|\\psi\\rangle\\).\n\nFrom the qubit Hamiltonian, two main circuit-based routes are shown. In one, \\(\\tilde H\\) is expanded into a sum of Pauli-like terms, and the time evolution is approximated by a product of exponentials of those pieces, i.e. a Trotterization procedure. This yields an approximate implementation of \\(e^{-it\\tilde H}|\\psi\\rangle\\) with a sequence of gates.\n\nIn the other route, \\(\\tilde H\\) is written as a linear combination of unitaries. An ancilla initialized in \\(|\\bar 0\\rangle\\) and several controlled blocks labeled by weights and unitary operations are used in a quantum signal processing framework. This also aims to approximate the same evolved state.\n\nOverall, the diagram compares two standard strategies for simulating fermionic time evolution on a quantum computer after converting the problem into qubit form.",
